answer=The paper measures library-learning compression by comparing the **compression metric** used for DreamCoder and Stitch, specifically the **“best-of-task” metric** in Eq. (18):

> “the compression metric in Eq. (18)”  
> “It is trivial to implement this best-of-task metric in Stitch, so we use this for the comparisons with DreamCoder.”

The metric is described as:

> “∑ cost(A) + min cost(Rewrite(p, A))” over tasks/programs

They then compare the resulting compression rates, shown as:

> “Rewritten corpus size ratio (DreamCoder / Stitch)”

and note:

> “Higher is better for Stitch: a ratio above 1.0 indicates that Stitch achieves greater compression than DreamCoder.”

So, in short, they judge whether compression improves on DreamCoder by **comparing the rewritten corpus size ratio under the Eq. (18) compression metric**.
